From 50f3f182fe9a6905565ec0993a4be74afe786e13 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sebastian Werner <swernerx@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 10:42:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(parser): answer the autolink pre-flight from the inline
 marker scan

On aarch64 the block-level inline parse no longer runs a separate
memchr2 + memmem pass over its content for the GFM autolink pre-flight.
The NEON marker classifier stops at `@` and `:` as well (both share
existing nibble-table bits, so it stays one shuffle pair), a `w.` pair
test adds five vector operations per 16 bytes, and every trigger found
is visited with the pre-flight's own per-position rule. A watermark
records which positions have been visited: bytes a construct consumed
without the marker scan classifying them (code spans, link text and
destinations, raw HTML) are visited before the next scan starts, and
whatever is left when the block finishes.

The result equals `may_contain_autolink` value for value, flags
included, so the output is identical by construction. Test builds assert
that gate value for every block they parse, and a differential test
compares AST Debug output and HTML under both pre-flights for the spec
fixtures, two frozen corpora and generated token soup. Other targets
keep the separate pass; the portable scalar tracker is tested everywhere.

Experimental: performance is to be measured with the paired harness.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
